+#### Voxel Data Retrieval for Unit Cube
+- **Step**: Initialize a `VoxelCorners<T>` struct. Iterate $i$ from 0 to 7. Calculate the world position of corner $i$ as $\text{localPosition} + \text{LookupTables.CubeCorners}[i]$. Attempt to retrieve the voxel data at this position from `voxelDataArray`. If successful, store it in `voxelDataCorners[i]` [S4, unofficial].
+- **Pitfall**: If `TryGetVoxelData` fails (e.g., out of bounds), the corresponding corner in `VoxelCorners` remains uninitialized (default value for T). This may lead to incorrect interpolation if not handled by the caller [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: $O(N)$ for $N$ points, as each point requires constant time (8 lookups) to collect corners [S1, S2, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity**: $O(N \cdot 8 \cdot C)$ to store the corner values for $N$ points with $C$ channels [G].
-- **Scaling**: Scales linearly with the number of points. Grid size does not affect lookup time, only memory bandwidth [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths**: Simple, differentiable, and efficient for dense grids [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations**: Does not handle non-axis-aligned grids (use [Curvilinear to Rectilinear Resampling](2248-curvilinear-to-rectilinear-resampling.md)) [S1, unofficial]. Requires boundary handling for points outside the grid [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to Use**: When trilinear interpolation is required on a regular grid [S1, S2, unofficial].
-- **When Not to Use**: When the grid is sparse (use [Sparse Voxel Octrees](link)) or non-axis-aligned (use [Curvilinear to Rectilinear Resampling](2248-curvilinear-to-rectilinear-resampling.md)) [S1, S2, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity**: $O(N)$ for $N$ points, as each point requires a constant number of operations (8 corner lookups) [S1, S2, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity**: $O(N)$ to store the corner indices and values for all points [S1, unofficial].
+- **Measured Performance**: On modern GPUs, SIMD-optimized variants can achieve high throughput by processing multiple points in parallel [S3, unofficial]. In neural rendering contexts, this method is often the bottleneck due to memory bandwidth constraints when accessing large grids [S1, unofficial].
+- **Scaling**: Scales linearly with the number of points and grid resolution (for memory access patterns). However, cache efficiency depends on the spatial coherence of the input points [S3, unofficial].
+- **Strengths**: Simple to implement, differentiable (if using continuous interpolation), and provides the necessary data for higher-order reconstruction [S1, S2, unofficial].
+- **Limitations**: Requires explicit boundary handling, which can be complex for non-uniform grids [S1, unofficial]. Performance may degrade if points are scattered and cause cache misses [S3, unofficial].
+- **When to Use**: When trilinear interpolation or surface extraction is required, and the grid is dense and regular [S1, S2, unofficial].
+- **When Not to Use**: For sparse grids where sparse data structures (e.g., Octrees) are more efficient [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from standard computer graphics techniques for sampling dense voxel data [S1, S2, unofficial]. Implementation details, such as the `VoxSample` struct layout and brick-based addressing logic, are documented in [S2, unofficial]. JAX-based variants with specific boundary handling are documented in [S3, unofficial].
+Grid Cell Collection via Corner Indices is a fundamental technique in volume rendering and computer graphics, originating from the need to sample dense voxel grids efficiently [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is widely used in neural rendering frameworks (e.g., Plenoxels) where differentiable trilinear interpolation is required for training neural fields [S1, unofficial]. The method has been adapted for various hardware architectures, including SIMD-optimized CPU implementations and GPU kernels for brick-structured volumes [S3, S1, unofficial].
 
